\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Complex Spin in dimension five is the symplectic group on spinors}

\p{Let #{V=W\oplus\mathbb Cz\oplus W'} be a five-dimensional complex
quadratic space with a supplied nondegenerate polarization, where
#{\dim W=2}, #{Q(z)=1}, and #{\rho(z)=\alpha}. On
#{S=\bigwedge W}, use the ordered basis

##{(1,e_1\wedge e_2,e_1,e_2).}

Define

##{\langle s,t\rangle=
[e_1\wedge e_2]\bigl(\operatorname{rev}_{\wedge}(s)\wedge t\bigr).}

Its matrix is #{\Omega=\operatorname{diag}(J,J)}, where
#{J=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}.
The pairing preserves exterior parity, so parity itself is self-adjoint.
Each creation operator and each polar-dual contraction operator is
self-adjoint as well. Consequently the faithful
even Clifford equivalence from \ref{fcap-002W} takes reversal to

##{A\longmapsto A^{\dagger}
=\Omega^{-1}A^{\mathsf T}\Omega}

\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivEnd}.
Reverse norm one is therefore exactly the symplectic equation
#{A^{\mathsf T}\Omega A=\Omega}.}

\p{This norm calculation alone does not yet prove membership in the
vector-generated Spin group. The five vector operators

##{c_1,\ c_2,\ a_1,\ a_2,\ \alpha}

are linearly independent, traceless, and #{\Omega}-self-adjoint. The
self-adjoint subspace of #{M_4(\mathbb C)} has dimension six, and its
trace-zero part has dimension five because #{\operatorname{tr}(I)=4}.
Hence the Clifford image of the vector space is exactly

##{\rho(V)=
\{T:T^{\dagger}=T,\ \operatorname{tr}(T)=0\}.}

Conjugation by every #{A\in Sp_{\Omega}(4,\mathbb C)} preserves this
subspace: symplectic conjugation preserves the adjoint condition, and
trace is conjugation-invariant.}

\p{The odd Clifford action is injective on the vector subspace. More
generally, if an odd Clifford value #{y} acts by zero, then #{zy} is
even and acts by zero. Faithfulness of the even action and invertibility
of #{z} give #{y=0}. Thus preservation of the displayed matrix subspace
lifts to preservation of #{V} inside the Clifford algebra.}

\p{For #{A\in Sp_{\Omega}(4,\mathbb C)}, let #{x} be its unique
preimage under the even Clifford equivalence. The adjoint equation gives
#{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1}, while the preceding calculation proves
#{xVx^{-1}=V}. The carrier-completion argument of \ref{fcap-0032} now places
#{x} in actual Spin. Conversely, every Spin element has symplectic
matrix because its reverse norm is one. These assignments are inverse
group maps, and the chosen exterior basis is the module intertwiner:

##{\Spin_5(\mathbb C)\simeq Sp_{\Omega}(4,\mathbb C),
\qquad S\simeq\mathbb C^4.}}

\p{Fulton--Harris state the same group and standard spin-module
identification
\citet{Exercise 20.39, p. 311}{fulton2004representation}.
Goodman--Wallach independently write the target as #{Sp(2,\mathbb C)},
the rank-two symplectic group on a four-dimensional complex space
\citet{Section 6.3.3, Exercise 1, p. 322}{goodman2009symmetry}.
The trace-zero normalization calculation supplies the actual group
inverse rather than inferring the image from a dimension count. By
contrast, Meinrenken's #{\Spin(5)=Sp(2)} is the compact quaternionic
real form
\citet{Section 3.7.6, pp. 83--84}{meinrenken2013clifford}.}

\p{TauCeti proves that in odd dimension the even Clifford action is an
equivalence onto the full endomorphism algebra and is faithful
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivEnd}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.evenSpinAction_injective}.
It does not package the invariant alternating form or the group image as
#{Sp_4}; those calculations are the explicit argument above.}
